Hot Partition Problem in Kafka

A hot partition happens when one Kafka partition receives much more traffic than the other partitions.

Example:

Topic has 3 partitions: P0, P1, P2
Most messages have the same key, e.g. customerId = 100
Kafka sends all those messages to P0
P0 becomes overloaded while P1 and P2 are mostly idle.

Problem: The hot partition becomes a bottleneck, causing high latency and reducing overall throughput.

Solutions:

Use a better/more distributed partition key
Increase the number of partitions
Avoid keys that have very low cardinality
For special high-volume keys, use key salting (e.g. customerId + random/suffix) if strict ordering for that key is not required.

Interview answer:

A hot partition occurs when one Kafka partition receives disproportionately high traffic compared to others.
It creates a bottleneck because the partition's consumer and broker resources become overloaded while other partitions remain underutilized.